  "notes": "South should win the opening lead as he may have to lose a diamond trick. South has 8 top tricks (2 spades, 3 hearts, and 3 clubs) outside of diamonds. South can afford to lose a trick in diamonds. South should thus gear his play towards maximizing his chances of losing only 1 diamond trick.\n\nIf South starts by leading a diamond to dummy's ♦10 or ♦Q, East may win cheaply leaving South with a critical guess on the second round of the suit. Should South finesse again or play for the drop of the outstanding honour?\n\nSouth can avoid this dilemma by taking North's ♦A on the first round of the suit. If both defenders follow small, South can return to his hand to play another diamond towards the dummy.\n\nBy playing diamonds in this way, South will succeed unless East has 3 or more diamonds including the ♦K and ♦J. In this case, the contract cannot be made.",
